WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:02.429
The company moves to a new building, with everyone free to choose a desk.

2
00:00:02.929 --> 00:00:06.877
Each person has just one small preference: at least half the people nearby should be in their department.

3
00:00:07.377 --> 00:00:10.363
A month later, the whole floor has split into two sections.

4
00:00:10.863 --> 00:00:12.614
Yet nobody had wanted that outcome.

5
00:00:13.514 --> 00:00:17.466
In 1971, economist Thomas Schelling explored this kind of process on a checkerboard.

6
00:00:18.366 --> 00:00:23.138
Equal numbers of two types of tokens are placed randomly, with some spaces left empty.

7
00:00:23.638 --> 00:00:27.114
At the start, roughly half of each token’s neighbors are the same type.

8
00:00:27.614 --> 00:00:32.435
There is one rule: if fewer than half your neighbors are the same type, move to the nearest satisfactory vacancy.

9
00:00:32.935 --> 00:00:36.788
One move leaves someone else dissatisfied. Moves continue round after round until everyone is satisfied.

10
00:00:37.688 --> 00:00:44.880
In one exercise, the proportion of same-type neighbors rose above four-fifths. Tokens with no unlike neighbors rose from roughly one in ten to four in ten.

11
00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:50.290
Requiring one-third produced only mild separation; at one-half, the separation was striking.

12
00:00:51.190 --> 00:00:58.912
He moved the pieces by hand, and there were not many runs. He said the figures applied only to this artificial checkerboard.

13
00:00:59.412 --> 00:01:06.147
His main concern was racial residential segregation in the US. Real life also involves exclusion and income differences, which the model deliberately leaves out.

14
00:01:07.047 --> 00:01:12.083
But it reminds us that a sharply divided overall pattern does not establish that every individual is strongly exclusionary.

15
00:01:12.983 --> 00:01:18.820
Have you seen a situation that seemed to separate naturally like this? Share your thoughts.

16
00:01:19.320 --> 00:01:21.109
Small preferences can accumulate into a much larger pattern.
